 4. 🎗️ Quote du jour: Bigger than cancer 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Susie Wiles attends a Rose Garden dinner on Sept. 2. Photo: Evelyn Hockstein/Reuters " Cancer can make your world very small. I worked hard not to let it," White House Chief of Staff Susie Wiles writes for USA Today about fighting breast cancer while holding one of the world's highest-pressure jobs:

 "The approach I took will not be right, or possible, for everyone. I have profound respect and empathy for every person enduring treatment for this disease. It drains you physically, mentally, emotionally and spiritually. For me, work provided something critically important: a mission larger than myself. … Work did not make the cancer disappear from my mind. However, it reminded me that I was still myself — and that my life was about more than my disease." "I hope sharing my story might offer some strength to even one person still in the fight," Wiles, who announced earlier this month that she's cancer-free, said on X . 

 "Yes, cancer  took something from me, but it also gave me a deeper appreciation for the people who carried me, the purpose that sustained me, and the preciousness of every day," her piece concludes. "I am grateful to every one of them. And, most especially, I am grateful to God." Read her essay . 

 
 
